+{"id":"8b8288cb-f467-4d91-8d9a-5f782ceea70e","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:12:38.766152","source":"agent","parent_id":"2c10e619-da65-40c0-8304-f09069607445","thought":[],"reasoning_content":"Now I understand the setup. The task: add `_orphan_entries_problem(orphan: dict, pred: dict) -> str | None`.\n\nThe orphan body has fields `{\"by\", \"log_index\", \"reason\", \"statement_b64\"}`. The `entries_b64` is presumably added to the orphan body. But check_body restricts allowed fields to `{\"by\", \"log_index\", \"reason\", \"statement_b64\"}` — should I add `entries_b64` to allowed? The task says `orphan[\"body\"][\"entries_b64\"]` is a list of base64-encoded canonical entry bytes. Presumably the ingest should accept it too, or maybe check_body already... it doesn't. Hmm, the task says \"add a function to record/record.py\" and \"wire it into wherever orphans are checked, so an orphan without valid hidden entries is reported.\"\n\n\"Orphan\" here means the orphaned attestation hid entries (the writer attested a range, then failed; the run's entries never got published, so the orphan must include the entries_b64 so the ledger can reconstruct them). Actually reading: an orphan attestation is a failure run; it attests a range; the entries that run produced must be hidden until... Actually the design: when a run fails, it attests entries it added; the entries become \"orphaned\" (not published to ledger branch) but Rekor has them, and an orphan record explains them. The orphan must list the entries_b64 it hid.\n\nSo `_orphan_entries_problem(orphan, pred)` checks that the hidden entries_b64 list, when decoded and canonicalized, matches the attested range exactly: count == to_seq - from_seq + 1, first chains from pred[\"prev_sha256\"] (its `prev` field), each next chains from previous sha256, last sha256 of canonical bytes == pred[\"head_sha256\"].\n\nWait — but the entries in the ledger chain have `prev` field and seq. The predicate's prev_sha256 is the sha256 of the entry before from_seq (chain[from_seq-2]). And head_sha256 is sha of entry at to_seq.\n\nReturn None if valid; else a short problem string: containing \"range\" when count wrong, \"chain\" when prev doesn't link, \"head\" when last hash mismatch.\n\nPriority/order: probably check range first, then chain, then head.\n\nNow \"wire it into wherever orphans are checked\": cmd_monitor, when checking an orphan against Rekor. After the statement check and head published check, we should also check `_orphan_entries_problem(o, pred)` and add to bad if it returns a string.\n\nAlso maybe check_body should allow \"entries_b64\"? The task says orphan[\"body\"][\"entries_b64\"] is a list of base64 strings. Since check_body currently rejects unknown fields, and the task presumably wants orphans with entries_b64 to be ingestible... Hmm, but the task only requires adding the function and wiring it in, and all existing tests must pass. The existing test `test_orphan_entries_explain_attestations` uses body without entries_b64 — `{\"by\": \"owner\", \"log_index\": 12345, \"reason\": ..., \"statement_b64\": \"e30=\"}`. If I add entries_b64 to allowed, that test still passes.\n\nShould I add \"entries_b64\" to the allowed set and validation? The function signature takes `orphan: dict, pred: dict` — presumably orphan is the full chain entry dict `o` (with o[\"body\"][\"entries_b64\"]). To make the feature coherent, yes: allow entries_b64 in orphan bodies, and the monitor uses it in the problem check.\n\nBut careful: adding validation could break existing tests? test_orphan_entries_explain_attestations proposes an orphan without entries_b64 — if entries_b64 is optional, fine.\n\nDesign of `_orphan_entries_problem`:\n\n```python\ndef _orphan_entries_problem(orphan: dict, pred: dict) -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"Check that the entries an orphan hid are exactly the range its attestation covered.\"\"\"\n    entries = orphan.get(\"body\", {}).get(\"entries_b64\") or []\n    try:\n        decoded = [base64.b64decode(x, validate=True) for x in entries]\n    except Exception:\n        return \"entries_b64 must be base64\"\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, but keep it simple and robust. Requirements:\n\n- expected count = pred[\"to_seq\"] - pred[\"from_seq\"] + 1\n- if len(decoded) != expected: return f\"range mismatch: attested {from}-{to} but {len} entries hidden\" — must contain \"range\".\n- then for each entry i: parse canonical bytes? They're \"base64-encoded canonical entry bytes (as produced by R.canonical(entry))\". So decode -> bytes; sha256 of bytes = hash; first entry's prev should equal pred[\"prev_sha256\"]; each next prev == previous sha256. Then last hash == pred[\"head_sha256\"].\n\nFor chain check: entry dict from json.loads(raw). prev field. First: pred[\"prev_sha256\"] (None if from_seq==1). Next: previous entry's sha256 (sha256 of its canonical bytes).\n\nFor head: sha256 of last decoded bytes == pred[\"head_sha256\"].\n\nReturn None if all good; else short problem string with the right keyword. Which takes precedence? Probably check range first (count wrong -> \"range\"). Then chain. Then head. Could an entry fail parse? If bytes aren't valid JSON... maybe return \"chain\" problem. Keep simple: parse failures -> maybe a generic message. Let's handle: if json parse fails, return \"entry bytes aren't canonical JSON\". Hmm, but requirements only list three cases. Keep it reasonable.\n\nImplementation:\n\n```python\ndef _orphan_entries_problem(orphan: dict, pred: dict) -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"An orphan's hidden entries must be exactly the range its attestation covered, else it launders a failed run.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        entries = [base64.b64decode(x, validate=True) for x in (orphan.get(\"body\") or {}).get(\"entries_b64\") or []]\n    except Exception:\n        return \"entries_b64 must be base64\"\n    from_seq, to_seq = pred.get(\"from_seq\"), pred.get(\"to_seq\")\n    if len(entries) != (to_seq - from_seq + 1):\n        return f\"attested range {from_seq}